        "\n",
        "# 13 Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "In this lesson we will learn how to write our own functions to reuse code. We will go over the syntax of a function, how to check its inputs, and a step-by-step strategy to turn code that works for one example into a function that works for any input.\n",
        "\n",
        "By the end of this lesson, students will be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- write functions with parameters, return values and docstrings\n",
        "- use `assert` to check a function's inputs\n",
        "- explain why a function should not depend on global variables\n",
        "- turn code that solves a concrete example into a reusable function\n",
        "\n",
        "## What are functions?\n",
        "\n",
        "In Python, **functions** are reusable blocks of code designed to perform specific tasks. They help streamline your code by allowing you to avoid repetition, improve readability, and make maintenance easier.\n",
        "\n",
        "The general syntax for defining a function is the following:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "def function_name(parameter_1, ..., parameter_n):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Docstring\"\"\"\n",
        "    <body of the function>\n",
        "    return value  # Depending on the function\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "We define a function using:\n",
        "\n",
        " 1. the `def` keyword, followed by the function name, parentheses (which can contain parameters), and a colon.\n",
        " 2. The first line(s) of the function should be a `docstring`. This is a string, written between triple quotes `\"\"\"`, that describes what the function does. It must be indented. Unlike a comment, Python stores the docstring with the function, so you can read it with `help(function_name)`.\n",
        " 3. After the docstring, you write the body of the function. This is the code that will be executed when the function is called. The whole body of the function should be indented to indicate the function's scope.\n",
        " 4. The `return` keyword is used to allow the function to return values. Functions that do not return any values don't need to have a `return` keyword.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's see two simple examples just to get familiar with the syntax. In the first one we have a simple function with a one-line docstring, no parameters, and no return values."
      ],
      "id": "2450007f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def greet():\n",
        "    \"\"\"Print a greeting message.\"\"\"\n",
        "    print(\"Hello, welcome to the class!\")\n",
        "\n",
        "greet()"
      ],
      "id": "b2041902",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The second one has a single parameter and a more detailed docstring with information about the arguments and return values."
      ],
      "id": "d82b8eca"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def calculate_area(radius):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    Calculate the area of a circle given its radius.\n",
        "\n",
        "    Args:\n",
        "        radius (float): The radius of the circle.\n",
        "\n",
        "    Returns:\n",
        "        float: The area of the circle, calculated as π * radius^2.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    area = 3.14159 * radius ** 2\n",
        "    return area\n",
        "\n",
        "# Using our function\n",
        "for radius in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]:\n",
        "    area = calculate_area(radius)\n",
        "    print(f\"The area of a circle with radius {radius} is {area:.2f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "e2966676",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "We can read the docstring of our function using `help()`:"
      ],
      "id": "db1bee5a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "help(calculate_area)"
      ],
      "id": "76ff7132",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Adding checks with `assert`\n",
        "\n",
        "Sometimes we want a function to stop and give a helpful message when it gets an input that doesn't make sense. We can do this with an `assert` statement, like we did to check our data in the [previous lesson](lesson-11-csv-to-geo.qmd):\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "assert <condition that should be True>, \"Message explaining what went wrong\"\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's update `calculate_area` so it gives an error when the radius is negative:"
      ],
      "id": "97170d60"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| error: true\n",
        "def calculate_area(radius):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    Calculate the area of a circle given its radius.\n",
        "\n",
        "    Args:\n",
        "        radius (float): The radius of the circle.\n",
        "\n",
        "    Returns:\n",
        "        float: The area of the circle, calculated as π * radius^2.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    # Check that radius is not negative\n",
        "    assert radius >= 0, f\"radius must be non-negative, got {radius}\"\n",
        "\n",
        "    area = 3.14159 * radius ** 2\n",
        "    return area\n",
        "\n",
        "# Check if the assertion works\n",
        "calculate_area(-2)"
      ],
      "id": "c0d83994",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In production code, input checks like this one are usually written with `raise` instead of `assert`, but `assert` works well at this stage. You can learn more about `raise` [here](https://realpython.com/python-raise-exception/). The core idea is that you can build in checks to test the inputs and outputs of your functions.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Global variables\n",
        "\n",
        "A **global variable** is a variable defined outside of any function, for example directly in a notebook cell. Functions can use global variables even if we don't pass them as parameters. Let's see why this can be a problem:"
      ],
      "id": "bbda16ea"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "threshold = 30   # °C\n",
        "\n",
        "def is_hot(temp):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Check if a temperature is above the threshold.\"\"\"\n",
        "    return temp > threshold\n",
        "\n",
        "print(is_hot(32))   # True\n",
        "\n",
        "# Later in the notebook, for another analysis...\n",
        "threshold = 50\n",
        "\n",
        "print(is_hot(32))   # False: same input, different answer!"
      ],
      "id": "e2f307fc",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`is_hot` uses the global variable `threshold`, so its result changed even though we passed the same temperature. We can fix this by making `threshold` a parameter:"
      ],
      "id": "f2d19ecc"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def is_hot(temp, threshold):\n",
        "    \"\"\"Check if a temperature is above a threshold.\"\"\"\n",
        "    return temp > threshold\n",
        "\n",
        "print(is_hot(32, 30))   # True\n",
        "print(is_hot(32, 50))   # False"
      ],
      "id": "d8a6d783",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Now the result depends only on what we pass to the function.\n",
        "\n",
        "**The best practice is for every variable used inside a function to be either a parameter or defined inside the function.**\n",
        "\n",
        "We'll come back to this when we write our own functions.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data loading and preparation\n",
        "\n",
        "We'll practice creating functions using the power plants and US states datasets from the [previous lesson](lesson-11-csv-to-geo.qmd)."
